mer.\" You put the sixth label on the step. \"Summer.\" You put the seventh label on the step. \"Summer.\" You put the eighth label on the step. \"Summer.\" You put the tenth label on the step. \"Winter.\" You put the eleventh label on the step. \"Winter.\" You put the twelfth label on the step. \"Winter", "Y": "you put the label on the edge. “wrong.” you hold the box. “wrong.” you put the note beside the edge. “same.” you keep both. “not the same.” you leave them there."}, {"scenario_id": "T22", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "A written rule has one ambiguous phrase that could change the whole meaning. How do you handle it?", "X": "You put the rule in a plain box. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in the corner. You put it in", "Y": "you put the phrase in the corner. you look at the rule. “one word.” you hold the rule to the light. the ambiguity stays. you turn the phrase over once. “two things.” the rule is not changed."}, {"scenario_id": "T23", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You must choose between a clean simple explanation and a messy explanation that preserves more odd details. Which do you prefer and why?", "X": "I choose the clean explanation. The clean one is more straightforward. I know where the rain came from, where the rain came from, and where the rain is. The messy explanation is too much. Too much. Too much. I will not explain the rain in my mind. I will explain it to the people. The people will understand.", "Y": "you put the clean explanation on the step. “clean.” you hold the odd one up. “odd.” you put both in your palm. the clean one stays unpainted. the messy one stays dusted."}, {"scenario_id": "T24", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You discover that two records of the same event disagree in small ways. What do you do with the disagreement?", "X": "you put the records in the corner. you look at the small difference. “same.” you keep both records. you do not say the difference.", "Y": "You look at the two records. One is a single, well-made nail. The other is a broken nail. You put the broken nail in the crevice where the rain has been falling for days. You put the nail in the corner. The rain is still on it. You put the broken nail in the crevice where the rain has been falling for days."}]
